WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.380
Welcome to the Ecology data workshop.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.240
Start with a population boundary and a time interval.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.060
A pond begins a month with two hundred fish.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.100
During that month there are forty births, fifteen deaths, ten immigrants and

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:13.880
five emigrants.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.120
Add births and immigration, then subtract deaths and emigration.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.320
The final count is two hundred thirty.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.600
The net increase is thirty fish, not forty births.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.780
Dividing thirty by the starting count gives a fifteen percent increase over

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:28.240
this month.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:32.920
That finite percentage is not automatically an instantaneous intrinsic growth

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:33.160
rate.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:37.720
If the problem excludes migration, state that assumption before dropping the

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:38.380
migration terms.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.980
Always check that all four event counts refer to the same population and

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:43.300
period.

00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:47.924
In the continuous exponential model, total growth rate equals the per capita

00:00:47.924 --> 00:00:50.924
rate r multiplied by population size N.

00:00:51.644 --> 00:00:56.544
If r is zero point two per year and N is one hundred, the instantaneous

00:00:56.544 --> 00:00:58.784
increase is twenty individuals per year.

00:00:59.604 --> 00:01:03.344
At N equal to three hundred, the same r gives sixty per year.

00:01:04.244 --> 00:01:07.684
Equal per capita rates therefore do not mean equal total increases.

00:01:08.484 --> 00:01:12.864
A negative r predicts decline in this model; zero predicts no net change.

00:01:13.804 --> 00:01:16.964
The exact continuous solution uses the exponential function.

00:01:18.104 --> 00:01:22.864
Multiplying N by one plus r is a discrete one step update, so do not silently

00:01:22.864 --> 00:01:24.624
substitute it for the continuous solution.

00:01:25.744 --> 00:01:30.226
Read whether a question asks for an instantaneous rate, a finite increase, or

00:01:30.226 --> 00:01:30.284
a

00:01:30.284 --> 00:01:30.904
future population.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.200
Now include a resource limit with the logistic model.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.480
Let carrying capacity K be one thousand and r be zero point four per year.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:44.563
At N equal to five hundred, the limiting factor one minus N over K is one

00:01:44.563 --> 00:01:44.860
half.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:48.720
Total growth is therefore one hundred individuals per year.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.740
At N equal to one hundred, total growth is thirty six.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.880
At N equal to nine hundred, it is also thirty six.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:00.600
For this simple continuous model with fixed positive r and fixed K, total

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.020
growth reaches its maximum at K over two.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.940
At K, net growth is zero, not necessarily zero births and deaths.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.920
Real populations can fluctuate, and changing resources can change K.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.460
The smooth curve is a model, not a guarantee.

00:02:14.736 --> 00:02:20.056
Suppose five nonoverlapping quadrats, each two square metres, contain eight,

00:02:20.056 --> 00:02:23.576
twelve, six, ten and fourteen plants of one species.

00:02:24.476 --> 00:02:26.756
The total is fifty plants across ten square metres.

00:02:27.596 --> 00:02:29.896
Estimated density is five plants per square metre.

00:02:30.816 --> 00:02:34.791
If the sample represents a two hundred square metre patch, multiplying gives

00:02:34.791 --> 00:02:34.896
an

00:02:34.896 --> 00:02:36.256
estimated one thousand plants.

00:02:37.156 --> 00:02:41.036
That extrapolation depends on representative placement and a clearly defined

00:02:41.036 --> 00:02:41.736
counting rule.

00:02:42.456 --> 00:02:45.676
Sampling only dense clumps biases the result upward.

00:02:46.976 --> 00:02:50.796
Repeated independent samples help describe variation; they do not repair a

00:02:50.796 --> 00:02:52.476
systematically biased placement rule.

00:02:53.676 --> 00:02:55.696
Density measures individuals per area or volume.

00:02:56.796 --> 00:03:00.696
Frequency instead asks how often the species occurs among sample units, and

00:03:00.696 --> 00:03:03.336
abundance within occupied quadrats answers another question.

00:03:03.696 --> 00:03:07.536
A vegetation plot fixes twelve hundred grams of carbon per square metre per

00:03:07.536 --> 00:03:09.836
year as gross primary production.

00:03:10.916 --> 00:03:13.396
Producer respiration returns five hundred in the same units.

00:03:14.296 --> 00:03:17.716
Net primary production is seven hundred grams of carbon per square metre per

00:03:17.716 --> 00:03:17.836
year.

00:03:18.476 --> 00:03:22.476
It represents the production remaining after producer respiration, available

00:03:22.476 --> 00:03:26.956
for plant growth, reproduction and transfer to consumers or detrital pathways.

00:03:27.536 --> 00:03:29.636
It is not all eaten by herbivores.

00:03:30.156 --> 00:03:34.416
Do not subtract total ecosystem respiration from gross production when the

00:03:34.416 --> 00:03:38.508
question asks for net primary production: that would include a different set

00:03:38.508 --> 00:03:38.616
of

00:03:38.616 --> 00:03:38.976
organisms.

00:03:39.816 --> 00:03:44.116
A standing biomass measurement is a stock at a time; primary production is a

00:03:44.116 --> 00:03:44.796
rate over time.

00:03:45.556 --> 00:03:48.196
Always carry the area and time units through the subtraction.

00:03:48.552 --> 00:03:52.502
A question supplies producer production of ten thousand kilojoules per square

00:03:52.502 --> 00:03:56.472
metre per year and herbivore production of eight hundred in the same units.

00:03:57.932 --> 00:04:00.532
Production transfer efficiency is eight percent.

00:04:00.532 --> 00:04:04.672
If the next transfer efficiency is specified as twelve percent, the next

00:04:04.672 --> 00:04:08.712
consumer level receives ninety six kilojoules per square metre per year as

00:04:08.712 --> 00:04:09.112
production.

00:04:10.232 --> 00:04:12.712
Multiply eight hundred by zero point twelve.

00:04:12.712 --> 00:04:15.292
Do not take twelve percent of the original producer value.

00:04:15.862 --> 00:04:19.694
The familiar ten percent rule is a teaching approximation, not a fixed

00:04:19.694 --> 00:04:20.132
measured

00:04:20.132 --> 00:04:21.652
efficiency for every ecosystem.

00:04:22.952 --> 00:04:26.232
Energy not incorporated into the next trophic level's production can enter

00:04:26.232 --> 00:04:29.072
respiration, uneaten material and waste pathways.

00:04:29.932 --> 00:04:32.572
Energy flows through the system, while matter can be recycled.

00:04:32.952 --> 00:04:38.052
An aquatic snapshot shows producer biomass of four grams per square metre and

00:04:38.052 --> 00:04:40.972
primary consumer biomass of twenty grams per square metre.

00:04:41.792 --> 00:04:44.032
The biomass pyramid is inverted at that moment.

00:04:45.072 --> 00:04:48.888
This does not mean consumers create energy or that production must also be

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:49.352
inverted.

00:04:50.532 --> 00:04:54.212
Small, rapidly replaced producers can supply consumers whose biomass

00:04:54.212 --> 00:04:55.352
accumulates for longer.

00:04:56.692 --> 00:04:58.892
Turnover links the stock to its rate of replacement.

00:04:59.562 --> 00:05:04.012
A pyramid of numbers can also be inverted, as when one tree supports many

00:05:04.012 --> 00:05:05.012
herbivorous insects.

00:05:05.912 --> 00:05:09.972
An energy pyramid compares energy flow or production over matching areas and

00:05:09.972 --> 00:05:13.352
time periods and remains upright in the standard trophic model.

00:05:14.452 --> 00:05:18.712
Before interpreting a diagram, read the axis label, units and sampling

00:05:18.712 --> 00:05:19.052
interval.

00:05:19.272 --> 00:05:23.252
Two identical litter samples lose different masses in two sites.

00:05:24.072 --> 00:05:26.532
The difference alone does not identify the cause.

00:05:27.552 --> 00:05:32.252
Temperature, moisture, oxygen, litter chemistry and decomposer communities may

00:05:32.252 --> 00:05:32.792
all differ.

00:05:33.712 --> 00:05:38.194
A stronger comparison uses replicated matched samples and changes the factor

00:05:38.194 --> 00:05:38.312
of

00:05:38.312 --> 00:05:41.132
interest while controlling other conditions as far as possible.

00:05:42.212 --> 00:05:46.646
Warm, moist, oxygenated conditions often favour decomposition, but

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:47.452
waterlogging

00:05:47.452 --> 00:05:50.432
can restrict oxygen and slow aerobic processes.

00:05:51.432 --> 00:05:55.212
Litter mass loss may reflect leaching and fragmentation as well as biological

00:05:55.212 --> 00:05:58.952
breakdown; it is not automatically a direct measurement of complete

00:05:58.952 --> 00:06:00.752
mineralisation.

00:06:00.752 --> 00:06:04.992
Humification produces relatively resistant humus, whereas mineralisation

00:06:04.992 --> 00:06:06.612
releases inorganic nutrients.

00:06:07.692 --> 00:06:10.752
Interpret the measurement that was actually made before naming a mechanism.

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:15.796
Two plots each contain ten species, so their recorded species richness is

00:06:15.796 --> 00:06:16.076
equal.

00:06:16.856 --> 00:06:20.596
If one plot is dominated by a single species while the other has similar

00:06:20.596 --> 00:06:23.396
abundances across species, their evenness differs.

00:06:24.296 --> 00:06:27.976
Equal richness therefore does not establish identical community diversity or

00:06:27.976 --> 00:06:28.296
function.

00:06:29.256 --> 00:06:31.816
Conservation questions also require careful categories.

00:06:32.636 --> 00:06:36.694
Endemic means naturally restricted to a particular geographic area; it does

00:06:36.694 --> 00:06:36.856
not

00:06:36.856 --> 00:06:38.336
mean a harmful introduced species.

00:06:38.336 --> 00:06:42.536
Protecting a species within its natural habitat is in situ conservation.

00:06:43.576 --> 00:06:46.596
Seed banks and suitable captive programmes are ex situ approaches.

00:06:46.596 --> 00:06:51.796
Neither label alone guarantees success: genetic representation, habitat

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:54.356
quality, threats and long term monitoring matter.

00:06:54.976 --> 00:06:59.091
A single declining count suggests a question to investigate, not proof that

00:06:59.091 --> 00:06:59.256
one

00:06:59.256 --> 00:07:00.636
named threat caused the decline.
